          "concept": "Next.js can render a page at build time (static) or on each request (dynamic), and it picks static whenever the page has no dynamic data. Static pages are fast and cacheable, while dynamic pages read fresh data such as cookies or search params on every visit. A page becomes dynamic the moment it uses cookies, headers, or searchParams, so one dynamic import can change a whole route. The debugging exercise below shows a typical slip with Next.js static and dynamic rendering — study it before you start the exercise.",
