          "concept": "Streams process data in chunks instead of loading it all into memory, which is how Node handles large files and network responses efficiently. A readable stream emits data events as chunks arrive, and piping connects a readable stream to a writable one with backpressure handled for you. Buffers hold raw binary data, and a chunk is usually a Buffer that you convert to a string with an encoding. The debugging exercise below shows a typical slip with Node.js streams and buffers — study it before you start the exercise.",
