          "introduction": "Middleware in Depth is a core skill in Express.js. Middleware is a function that runs between the request arriving and the route handling it, and it can log, authenticate, parse, or end the request. In this lesson you will practise Express middleware with small, runnable examples.",
          "concept": "Middleware is a function that runs between the request arriving and the route handling it, and it can log, authenticate, parse, or end the request. Each middleware receives req, res, and next, and it must call next() to hand control to the next function in the chain. app.use registers middleware for every request, while passing it to a single route limits it to that route. The debugging exercise below shows a typical slip with Express middleware — study it before you start the exercise.",
          "syntax": "app.use((req, res, next) => {",
          "analogy": "Express middleware works like a post office sorting desk: every item is checked, labelled, and sent to the right place.",
          "exampleCode": "app.use((req, res, next) => {\n  console.log(req.method, req.url);\n  next();\n});",
          "exampleLanguage": "javascript",
