          "introduction": "Authentication with Middleware is a core skill in Express.js. Authentication in Express is usually middleware: it reads a token from the Authorization header, verifies it, and either attaches the user to req or answers 401. In this lesson you will practise Express authentication middleware with small, runnable examples.",
          "concept": "Authentication in Express is usually middleware: it reads a token from the Authorization header, verifies it, and either attaches the user to req or answers 401. Protected routes list the middleware before their handler, so unauthenticated requests never reach the logic. Keeping the check in one middleware means every route gets the same rule, instead of each handler re-implementing it. The debugging exercise below shows a typical slip with Express authentication middleware — study it before you start the exercise.",
          "syntax": "function requireAuth(req, res, next) {",
