          "id": "laravel-m3-l3",
          "slug": "laravel-m3-l3",
          "title": "Model Relationships",
          "objectives": [
            "Explain what Laravel model relationships means and when to use it",
            "Apply Laravel model relationships in a small working example",
            "Recognise and avoid common mistakes with Laravel model relationships"
          ],
          "introduction": "Model Relationships is a core skill in Laravel. Eloquent relationships link models: a Post belongsTo an Author, and an Author hasMany Posts. In this lesson you will practise Laravel model relationships with small, runnable examples.",
          "concept": "Eloquent relationships link models: a Post belongsTo an Author, and an Author hasMany Posts. Defining the relationship on the model lets you write $post->author and $author->posts, and eager loading with with('author') fetches the related rows in one query instead of one per row. The foreign key lives on the belonging side, which is the detail that trips most beginners. The debugging exercise below shows a typical slip with Laravel model relationships — study it before you start the exercise.",
          "syntax": "class Post extends Model {",
