          "introduction": "Replacing jQuery with Vanilla JS is a core skill in jQuery & the DOM. Modern browsers have absorbed jQuery's conveniences: querySelector replaces $, classList replaces addClass, addEventListener replaces on, and fetch replaces $.get. In this lesson you will practise Replacing jQuery with vanilla JS with small, runnable examples.",
          "concept": "Modern browsers have absorbed jQuery's conveniences: querySelector replaces $, classList replaces addClass, addEventListener replaces on, and fetch replaces $.get. Replacing jQuery is mostly mechanical — one method at a time — and the result is smaller, with no dependency to update. The ideas survive the translation: selection, traversal, events, and Ajax are the same concepts in both. The debugging exercise below shows a typical slip with Replacing jQuery with vanilla JS — study it before you start the exercise.",
