          "concept": "Headings give a page its outline: one h1 for the page title, then h2 for sections and h3 for subsections, in order and without skipping levels. Screen reader users navigate by headings the way sighted users scan a page, so a skipped level or a heading chosen for its size breaks that map. Headings are structure, not styling — size comes from CSS, meaning comes from the level. The debugging exercise below shows a typical slip with Accessible headings and structure — study it before you start the exercise.",
